<section>
  <p>Godzilla is built from three ideas that compose into everything else in these docs. Understanding how they relate makes the rest of the platform — guides, API reference, SDKs — much easier to follow.</p>

  {{ds:component/card|href={{cms:item/1c05e59d-f366-4176-a6ca-8f52ccfb3ce6}}|tag=Concept|title=Pipelines|description=Named, versioned workflows that move data from a source to a destination through optional transforms.}}
  {{ds:component/card|href={{cms:item/9abaed7a-d8e0-4359-8beb-09b323107b37}}|tag=Concept|title=Connectors|description=Pre-built integrations to external systems — sources, destinations, or both.}}
  {{ds:component/card|href={{cms:item/51573947-e8c4-48fb-9b1d-148c2a99aa85}}|tag=Concept|title=Events|description=The atomic unit of data flowing through a pipeline, plus the platform's own lifecycle events.}}
  {{ds:component/card|href={{cms:item/132b7ae5-caad-4f00-a0dd-779f9a64c41c}}|tag=Concept|title=Schemas|description=How to declare and validate event payload shapes using JSON Schema.}}
</section>

<section>
  <h2>How they fit together</h2>
  <p>A <strong>pipeline</strong> reads <strong>events</strong> from a source <strong>connector</strong>, transforms them, and writes them to a destination connector. Everything else in Godzilla — retries, webhooks, monitoring — is built on top of that single loop. <strong>Schemas</strong> let you declare and enforce the shape of those events for data quality and safety.</p>
</section>

